## Benchmark Parquet Writers

Measure whether the parquet writers keep their content defined chunking state
across row groups, for both writing a whole table with a single call and
streaming it in batches, write calls inside the row groups. pyarrow writes each
chunk of a table with a column writer call of its own, inside the row groups, so
parquet-cpp streams a table whose chunks are the batches:

```bash
❯ de bench runs/stock
```

This generates 16Mi rows of `{"a": "int"}` into `runs/stock/sources` together
with versions having 10×10 rows inserted and deleted. Then every writer
(`parquet-cpp` via pyarrow, `parquet-rs` via arrow-rs) rewrites them with row
groups of 1Mi, 256Ki and 64Ki rows, and streamed in batches of 100000 rows.
It shows three tables:

- page cuts against each writer's single row group file without page size
  limits: a writer keeping its chunker state across row groups reproduces all of
  those cuts (recall 100%, extra 0, aligned 100%, resync 0)
- deduplication of the original with its inserted and deleted versions, for
  PLAIN and dictionary encoding and without CDC as a baseline
- page cuts of the same data written by different implementations

Results are saved to `runs/stock/results.json`. To compare with another
environment, for example a pyarrow built from a different Arrow branch, reuse
the same sources there and compare the runs:

```bash
❯ de bench runs/cdc-branch --sources runs/stock/sources --writer parquet-cpp
❯ de compare runs/stock runs/cdc-branch
```

A single edit early in a larger file, e.g. 10 rows inserted and deleted at 24.9%
of a ~1GB file with 13 to 3200 row groups, shifting three quarters of the data.
The page cut profiles need a single row group oracle which pyarrow limits to 64Mi
rows, so only the deduplication profiles are run:

```bash
❯ de bench runs-1g/stock --num-rows 209715200 --edit-point 0.249 \
    --row-group-size 16777216 --row-group-size 4194304 --row-group-size 1048576 \
    --row-group-size 262144 --row-group-size 65536 \
    --profile default --profile dict --profile nocdc --max-workers 8
```

parquet-java is opt-in: `de/java/ParquetRewrite.java` rewrites the sources with it
and needs the classpath of parquet-hadoop, e.g. from a parquet-java checkout:

```bash
❯ ./mvnw -q -DskipTests -pl parquet-hadoop -am package dependency:build-classpath \
    -Dmdep.outputFile=cp.txt -Dmdep.includeScope=compile
❯ export PARQUET_JAVA_CLASSPATH=$PWD/parquet-hadoop/target/parquet-hadoop-1.19.0-SNAPSHOT.jar:$(cat parquet-hadoop/cp.txt)
❯ de bench runs/java --writer parquet-cpp --writer parquet-rs --writer parquet-java
```

DuckDB is opt-in as well: a `COPY ... TO ... (FORMAT parquet)` statement rewrites the
sources with the binary at `DUCKDB_BINARY`. Content defined chunking needs a build of
[duckdb#27911](https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/pull/27911). DuckDB pages have no row
limit, so it skips the `aligned` profile:

```bash
❯ gh pr checkout 27911 && GEN=ninja make reldebug
❯ export DUCKDB_BINARY=$PWD/build/reldebug/duckdb
❯ de bench runs/duckdb --writer parquet-cpp --writer parquet-rs --writer duckdb
```

Show the exact pages of a file, or compare the pages of two files:

```bash
❯ de pages runs/stock/cut/parquet-cpp/original-snappy-cdc-256Ki-1Mi-n0-nodict-rg-64Ki-dp-1Gi-rpp-1Gi.parquet
❯ de page-diff \
    runs/stock/cut/parquet-cpp/original-snappy-cdc-256Ki-1Mi-n0-nodict-rg-64Ki-dp-1Gi-rpp-1Gi.parquet \
    runs/stock/cut/parquet-rs/original-snappy-cdc-256Ki-1Mi-n0-nodict-rg-64Ki-dp-1Gi-rpp-1Gi.parquet
```

## Parity

Check where the writers write exactly the same pages as parquet-cpp: every writer
writes a table per datatype, each of the types in `de/parity.py` with and without
nulls, with the settings of each case (PLAIN in a single row group, row groups, a
row or size limit per page, dictionary, snappy and zstd), with both v1 and v2 data
pages. A type a writer can't write as the same parquet type is n/a, as is a case it
can't express. A cell is identical, has cuts in only one of the files,
or has pages with the same cuts but different bytes (headers aside). The files are
kept to inspect any cell with `de page-diff`:

```bash
❯ de parity runs/parity --writer parquet-rs --writer parquet-java --writer duckdb
❯ de page-diff runs/parity/v1/plain/parquet-cpp/list-int-cdc-*.parquet runs/parity/v1/plain/parquet-java/list-int-cdc-*.parquet
```

With v2 data pages every writer uses the encodings of writer version 2.0, the only
ones parquet-java writes v2 pages with: values that aren't dictionary encoded are
delta encoded if they are integers or byte arrays, parquet-cpp is told so per
column. DuckDB writes no v2 data pages, its `PARQUET_VERSION V2` only changes the
encodings.

The parquet spec says nothing about content defined chunking, nor about writers
writing the same bytes, so the known differences in the payloads are all valid
parquet:

| Difference | Writer | Cause |
|---|---|---|
| Level encoding | parquet-java | Runs of fewer than 8 repeated levels are bit-packed where parquet-cpp writes RLE runs, the decoded levels are the same. Mostly in nested columns. |
| Dictionary | parquet-java | Falls back to PLAIN (delta with v2 pages) for the whole column chunk unless the dictionary and the indices of its first page are smaller than PLAIN. parquet-cpp keeps the dictionary until the dictionary page reaches 1 MiB. |
| Dictionary, v2 | parquet-rs | Falls back to delta encodings, parquet-cpp to PLAIN as pyarrow can't combine `column_encoding` with dictionary encoding. |
| Delta encoding, v2 | parquet-java | DELTA_BINARY_PACKED blocks of 128 INT64 values instead of 256, and stale bits instead of zeros in the padding and the bit widths of unused miniblocks in the last block. Every integer and string page differs. |
| Snappy | parquet-java, parquet-rs | Different libraries, the decompressed pages are the same: snappy-java 1.1.10 and the pure Rust `snap` crate. |
| Zstd | parquet-java | Level 3 instead of 1, the streaming API (no content size in the frame header) and a flush before closing (an empty last block). |
| Compression, v2 | parquet-java, parquet-rs | v2 pages compress the values only. parquet-cpp leaves them uncompressed unless that makes them smaller, parquet-rs unless they get smaller than the levels and values together, parquet-java always compresses them. |

The page headers aren't compared, they differ too:

| Header field | parquet-cpp | parquet-rs | parquet-java |
|---|---|---|---|
| Statistics | In every data page header unless a page index is written, so `de` writes one | None | None |
| CRC | None | None | By default, `de` turns it off |
| Repetition level encoding of flat columns, v1 | RLE | RLE | BIT_PACKED |
| Dictionary encodings, v1 | PLAIN, RLE_DICTIONARY | PLAIN, RLE_DICTIONARY | PLAIN_DICTIONARY |
| `is_compressed`, v2 | Whether the values are compressed | Whether the values are compressed | Always true, even uncompressed |

Deduplication across writers needs the same bytes, headers included: xet chunks
(64 KiB on average) span several pages, so a single different header byte makes a
chunk new. The xet dedup ratio of the parity tables written by parquet-cpp (pyarrow
26.0.1) and another writer, 0.5 is complete deduplication and 1.0 none:

| Writer | Pages | plain | row-groups | row-limit | size-limit | dictionary | snappy | zstd |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| parquet-rs 58.4.0 | v1 | 0.515 | 0.625 | 0.518 | 0.988 | 0.518 | 0.565 | 0.523 |
| parquet-rs 58.4.0 | v2 | 0.516 | 0.599 | 0.519 | 0.996 | 0.930 | 0.685 | 0.553 |
| parquet-java 1.19.0-SNAPSHOT | v1 | 0.913 | 0.918 | 0.913 | 0.998 | 0.939 | 0.948 | 0.999 |
| parquet-java 1.19.0-SNAPSHOT | v2 | 0.996 | 0.996 | 0.996 | 0.998 | 1.000 | 0.997 | 1.000 |

parquet-rs writes parquet-cpp's bytes with both page versions, except for its wrong
cuts of nested columns after the first row group and its empty pages at a size
limit. parquet-java writes them only with v1 pages of nested columns where the level
encoding agrees.

# Results on Synthetic Data

The experiment can be reproduced by running the following command:

```bash
❯ de synthetic -s $S -e $E '{"a": "int"}'
```

This will generate `$S` million (2^20) records of schema `<a: int>` with variants
including `$E` edits (inserted/deleted/updated block of 10 records) evenly
distributed in the table and another variant with 5% of the records appended to
the end. The resulting parquet files have `$S` row groups.

The tool also generates .jsonlines files for comparison since content defined
chunking is well suited for row major formats.

